1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,848
A developer on a locked-down PC wants an AI pair programmer.

2
00:00:04,848 --> 00:00:11,394
Cloud assistants are blocked, and nobody wants project files leaving the machine.

3
00:00:11,394 --> 00:00:23,272
Phoenix LAi runs Gemma models on your own GPU, serves them as an OpenAI API, and gives you a chat that edits project files only where you allow it.

4
00:00:23,272 --> 00:00:26,828
Everything you will see is running for real.

5
00:00:27,078 --> 00:00:30,004
Phoenix is two programs on one PC.

6
00:00:30,004 --> 00:00:37,252
The server turns Gemma 4 models into an OpenAI API on port 1234, with a dashboard and a console.

7
00:00:37,744 --> 00:00:39,279
Models are plain files in a models folder.

8
00:00:39,979 --> 00:00:42,642
The engine runs them on your GPU through the driver's own Vulkan.

9
00:00:43,358 --> 00:00:46,682
No installs, no admin.

10
00:00:47,471 --> 00:00:50,462
The client is the chat window.

11
00:00:50,462 --> 00:00:59,477
It holds your project files in a jailed folder, with a backup before every edit, and talks to the server over the same API.

12
00:01:00,177 --> 00:01:06,739
Providers are any OpenAI compatible address: the Phoenix server, Fairlead, LM Studio, Ollama, even a cloud.

13
00:01:06,739 --> 00:01:10,524
Only the Phoenix server gets your files by default.

14
00:01:10,524 --> 00:01:12,366
Everyone else gets what you type.

15
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:17,462
Cloud keys live in Lazarette and are shared to Fairlead.

16
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:19,893
Phoenix never reads the vault.

17
00:01:19,893 --> 00:01:24,562
It holds only its own Fairlead key, in Windows Credential Manager.

18
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:30,925
A VS Code panel shows the same chat, with inline completion from the server.

19
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:38,854
And the safety line: the model never runs a command, every edit keeps the old version, and your files stay on this PC.

20
00:01:38,854 --> 00:01:41,840
This is the Phoenix server's dashboard, live.

21
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:45,047
No model is loaded and no GPU memory is in use.

22
00:01:45,047 --> 00:01:50,719
One client is connected, the Phoenix client, with its last call listed.

23
00:01:50,719 --> 00:01:52,063
Everything here is local.

24
00:01:52,763 --> 00:01:57,334
The console speaks only Phoenix's own commands, never a shell.

25
00:01:57,334 --> 00:02:00,150
Status shows the API address and the device.

26
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:02,847
Models lists what the API offers.

27
00:02:02,847 --> 00:02:04,482
Here, no models folder yet.

28
00:02:05,346 --> 00:02:06,146
This PC has no Gemma 4 file, and Phoenix refuses other models.

29
00:02:06,366 --> 00:02:12,681
So for this demo, the model comes through Fairlead.

30
00:02:12,931 --> 00:02:18,322
The client opens, and the Fleet skin switches it to navy and brass.

31
00:02:18,322 --> 00:02:24,260
Providers lists the Phoenix server, green because it is running.

32
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:25,660
Now we add Fairlead.

33
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:27,160
Plus.

34
00:02:26,674 --> 00:02:34,149
A name, Fairlead's local address, and the key Fairlead issued to this app.

35
00:02:34,149 --> 00:02:39,565
It goes straight into Windows Credential Manager, never into a file.

36
00:02:40,419 --> 00:02:45,180
Load models: Fairlead offers its routes, here one called local.

37
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:51,620
Test probes eighteen OpenAI features, so Phoenix never sends this provider something it refuses.

38
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:56,053
The feature map is saved with the provider.

39
00:02:56,053 --> 00:03:01,154
Embeddings are not offered, so docs search falls back to keywords.

40
00:03:01,154 --> 00:03:04,955
And look at the privacy switch: off.

41
00:03:04,955 --> 00:03:10,539
This provider gets only what you type, never your files.

42
00:03:10,539 --> 00:03:11,339
Save.

43
00:03:11,637 --> 00:03:20,096
First live answer: the question goes through Fairlead to a three billion parameter Llama on this machine, about ninety tokens a second.

44
00:03:20,096 --> 00:03:25,618
The answer is rough, as small models are, but it is real and it is live.

45
00:03:26,230 --> 00:03:29,812
Scenario: a ledger clerk with a small PowerShell script.

46
00:03:29,812 --> 00:03:33,835
A project is a folder, jailed inside Phoenix projects.

47
00:03:33,835 --> 00:03:39,846
I drop in two synthetic files, a script and a sample ledger, and ask about the script.

48
00:03:40,472 --> 00:03:44,717
With the privacy switch off, Fairlead never saw the file.

49
00:03:44,717 --> 00:03:47,737
The model can only guess, and it does.

50
00:03:47,737 --> 00:03:50,611
That is the gate working, not a bug.

51
00:03:51,311 --> 00:03:56,037
To let this provider see the project, open its window and switch on send local context.

52
00:03:56,037 --> 00:04:00,409
The red warning says project files are sent to this provider.

53
00:04:00,409 --> 00:04:04,288
Fairlead's local route stays on this PC, so I accept it.

54
00:04:04,288 --> 00:04:05,088
Save.

55
00:04:05,259 --> 00:04:12,560
Now the same chat lists the project with a real list command, and finds the script, the ledger and a rules file.

56
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:19,754
A deliberately mechanical edit for a small model: replace this text with the decimal cast.

57
00:04:19,754 --> 00:04:27,032
Phoenix shows the diff, writes it, and keeps the previous version in a dot llm backup folder.

58
00:04:27,032 --> 00:04:31,052
A small model can get this wrong, so the backup is the point.

59
00:04:31,384 --> 00:04:32,184
Safety.

60
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:34,717
The model never runs anything.

61
00:04:34,717 --> 00:04:40,152
I ask it to run the script, and all it does is give me the command to run myself.

62
00:04:40,912 --> 00:04:45,125
Ask again, even with permission, and the run guard steps in.

63
00:04:45,638 --> 00:04:50,240
A red warning, and the chat says this is the third request to run something.

64
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:55,529
The sixth locks chat input for five minutes, and the lockout survives a restart.

65
00:04:55,529 --> 00:04:57,204
I will not push it.

66
00:04:58,095 --> 00:05:02,183
The project jail: I try to open a folder outside Phoenix projects.

67
00:05:02,183 --> 00:05:09,115
Dots in the path are refused, and the message says folders are always inside the projects folder, fixed and unchangeable.

68
00:05:09,815 --> 00:05:13,347
Same answer for a project path that tries to climb out with dot dot.

69
00:05:13,347 --> 00:05:17,233
Everything in the client is a slash command, no menus.

70
00:05:17,233 --> 00:05:25,263
Help lists them all: connect a project, switch provider, compact a long chat, accept mode to approve each file action.

71
00:05:25,963 --> 00:05:32,058
Skills are reusable instructions in plain files, matched to what you type or called by name.

72
00:05:32,058 --> 00:05:35,006
The Skill Builder drafts and tests them.

73
00:05:35,256 --> 00:05:36,762
The evidence trail.

74
00:05:36,762 --> 00:05:42,629
The chat is saved with the project, and the edit line names the exact backup folder holding the version from before the change.

75
00:05:43,329 --> 00:05:49,542
Open the saved chat and every step is there: the list, the diff, the write and the backup path.

76
00:05:50,242 --> 00:05:54,127
Back on the server dashboard, the only requests are the client's embedding checks.

77
00:05:54,127 --> 00:05:56,506
The chat itself went through Fairlead.

78
00:05:57,006 --> 00:06:00,616
Phoenix LAi keeps the work on the machine.

79
00:06:00,616 --> 00:06:09,211
Your files go only where you allow, the model never runs a command, and every edit keeps its backup.

80
00:06:09,211 --> 00:06:15,485
Today's model came through Fairlead, because this PC has no Gemma 4 file.

81
00:06:15,485 --> 00:06:18,322
Everything else you saw was live.
